///*Abstract - `pygrametl` is a Python framework for building extract-transform-load (ETL) workflows. Its existing parallel implementation uses separate processes to bypass the Global Interpreter Lock (GIL). This paper evaluates whether newer Python concurrency mechanisms and PostgreSQL pipeline mode can reduce these costs. Additionally, multiprocessing, asynchronous extraction, free-threaded CPython, and subinterpreters are compared. Subinterpreters are assessed with microbenchmarks, while the other approaches are tested on a star-schema workload. At the largest size, multiprocessing reduces execution time from 113.6 s to 55.5 s, and pipelining reduces it further to 36.8 s sequentially. The fastest configuration combines pipelining with free-threading, completing in 17.5 s. Subinterpreters offer no clear enough advantage in the measured data transfer workloads to justify replacing multiprocessing. Overall, database round trips are the most consequential target in this workload.*/
